WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.470
China is the most polluting country in the
world, as well as the largest energy consumer.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:10.780
Surely that comes as no surprise, right?

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:18.460
Well that’s exactly why the Asian superpower
plays a key role when it comes to meeting

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:23.319
the objectives of the Paris Agreement, which
seeks to limit global warming by a maximum

00:00:23.319 --> 00:00:26.789
of 2oC.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:34.200
And to achieve this goal, decreasing the use
of coal to produce energy is fundamental.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.860
The Paris Agreement also stipulates that the
combustion of coal should be eliminated worldwide

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.640
by the year 2050.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:49.960
Well, China currently emits 30% of the world’s
CO2 emissions, even though it only accounts

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.200
for 20% ​​of all global manufacturing.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.870
This means that China is still very inefficient
when it comes to managing pollution.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:06.280
And this has a lot to do with its huge dependence
on coal to fuel its power plants.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.009
Coal is a very cheap way of producing energy
and China has massive coal reserves.

00:01:11.009 --> 00:01:14.770
And although, it wasn’t too long ago that
these sooty plants had a clearly scheduled

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:17.560
end date, that isn’t so clear anymore.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.090
Dear friends of VisualPolitik.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.600
It seems that China has sneakily gone back
on the promise that was recently made by Xi

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.799
Jinping himself.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:34.069
Or ... at least, they’ve halted progress.

00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:38.219
In this video we’re going to tell you how
and why China has decided to overlook the

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:45.600
pollution problems caused by using coal and
what can be expected as a result of this decision.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.579
Listen up!

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:53.069
(INTRO)

00:01:53.069 --> 00:01:54.310
Check out these news stories.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.189
They’re only three years apart.

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:00.950
(2016 - China warns Trump against abandoning
climate change deal - Financial Times)

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:06.149
(2019 - Years after freezing new projects,
China is back to building coal power plants

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:07.149
- The Washington Post)

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:08.149
Surprised?

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:09.149
Well... you shouldn't be.

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:11.040
Xi Jinping isn’t exactly great at leading
by example.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.230
It’s true that some progress has been made,
but China’s current relationship with coal

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:22.690
is generating many questions.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:27.350
China is still a developing country and therefore
its demand for energy – which is not only

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:32.300
driven by industry but also by its steadily
growing middle-class – is unstoppable.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.750
China literally devours energy.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.170
The Asian superpower is still far from reaching
Europe’s position, where with cleaner energy

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.500
and efficiency improvements, we can aim to
keep both energy consumption and CO2 emissions

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.500
at bay.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.090
Meanwhile the trend in China is quite the
opposite.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:54.090
In fact, despite all the promises and the
government’s many plans, this country is

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.900
not only failing to decrease its coal-based
energy production, it’s actually increasing

00:02:58.900 --> 00:02:59.900
it.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.740
And with that, its emissions will increase
as well.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.860
Yes, I’m saying that China’s carbon-linked
emissions are growing every year.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:10.580
Do you want more details?

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.070
Listen up.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:16.400
(CHINA AND COAL, A FATEFUL MATCH?)

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.950
There’s a determining fact in China's relationship
with coal:

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:24.310
These days, China simply cannot – and does
not want to – stop producing energy from

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.480
coal.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.430
As we explained at the beginning: The great
Asian giant has once again approved the construction

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.990
of coal thermoelectric plants in at least
11 provinces.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.710
We aren’t talking about one, two, or even
ten new plants.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:43.600
We’re talking about at least two dozen new
plants, some of them huge, which will increase

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.280
China’s coal-based energy production by
150 gigawatts.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.760
To put that into perspective, that’s equivalent
to the coal-based energy production of the

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.890
entire European Union.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.190
And it doesn’t end there.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.760
To supply all these new plants, 40 new coal
mines will be opened in the upcoming years.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.960
That is, thousands of jobs will be created
in this industry.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.230
And this will happen within just a couple
of years.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:08.740
Now, hold on just a second.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:13.569
Does this mean that China has definitely turned
its back on its commitment to reduce pollution?

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:15.510
Well no, not entirely.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.060
At least, as long as we’re talking in relative
terms.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:29.380
Between 2012 and 2018, the percentage of coal-based
energy in the energy mix dropped from 68.5%

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.740
to 59%.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.560
That means that in just six years, China went
from producing more than two-thirds of all

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:38.900
its energy needs from coal to just over half
of it.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:43.510
It’s still a huge percentage, but it’s
also a considerable reduction and that’s

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.700
the statistic that the government tends to
boast about.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:54.220
But of course, the fact that China decreased
its coal-dependence by 10 percentage points...

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.470
is no cause for celebration.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:59.710
In fact… does relative data really matter
when we’re talking about pollution?

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:04.250
Why should the mix distribution matter if
carbon emissions continue to increase?

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:05.460
Think about it for a moment.

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.770
Imagine that China’s total energy production
for one year is 1000 units of energy and a

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.430
few years later it rises to 1500.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:18.430
Well, assume that the first 700 of those 1000
units were produced with coal, and years later

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.680
they were producing 900 out of 1500 with coal.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.600
Technically, the percentage of coal in their
total energy production would’ve decreased,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.990
but in reality, coal energy production would’ve
increased by 200 units.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:34.040
And when we’re talking about pollution,
emissions and the environment, what matters

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:35.890
are the absolute figures.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.380
And that’s precisely what’s happening
in China.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.750
Check this out.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:44.950
(CHART)

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:50.290
As you can see, since 2000 China has multiplied
its coal-based energy production by five,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.580
while in the same period, the United States
and the European Union have reduced theirs

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.580
significantly.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.110
And yes, okay, some may think...

00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:02.180
But Simon if we measure it in per capita terms
then China isn’t the most polluting country.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:06.699
Well, yes, you are absolutely right, it’s
the same issue as we had when we considered

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:07.780
the energy mix.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.240
When we talk about pollution, what matters
is absolute figures and how they evolve.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.090
And as you can see, China isn’t only the
largest polluter in the world, it’s emissions

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:18.600
are also on the rise.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:25.490
Now, just a second, before we pass a verdict.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.889
What would you say if I told you that China,
besides being a world leader in dirty energy,

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:32.850
is also the world leader in clean energy?

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:37.610
Well... it may be hard to get your head around
but it’s true.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.990
In absolute figures, China produces more energy
with clean sources than any other country

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:47.050
on the planet.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:53.120
In 2018, they produced 728 gigawatts with
renewable energy, 12% more than in 2017.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.840
But... how is this possible?

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.169
Why on earth are they still building coal
plants instead of focusing on new technology?

00:06:59.169 --> 00:07:02.680
Well, the truth is that there’s a lot of
internal conflict on this issue.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.780
Because China literally floats on coal.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:10.070
Its subsoil is full of this mineral.

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:13.750
Without coal, some regions would return to
absolute poverty overnight.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:16.449
We’re talking about thousands of workers,
hundreds of thousands of workers who make

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:23.490
a living from this sector: miners, power plant
workers, transporters, etc.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.930
Therefore, the problem lies in what would
happen in China if the domestic coal market

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.090
was eliminated.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.910
Nowadays it’s an essential sector that produces
low-cost coal that can then be sold competitively

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:39.720
in international markets.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.860
And that’s where the big question arises,
the key question: Will China be able to meet

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.880
the international community’s deadlines
to combat greenhouse gas emissions?

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:53.160
Will it really be able to kick its dependence
on coal?

00:07:53.160 --> 00:08:00.080
(THE CHINESE PARADIGM: FROM AMBITION TO REALITY)

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.639
In the case of China, things are a bit more
complicated than they seem.

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:07.210
As you can see, in this country, giving up
polluting plants and relying on renewable

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.290
energy sources isn’t that simple.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:13.250
But this problem isn’t a new one, it started
a long time ago.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:21.400
In the 1980s, as you all know, China experienced
a fierce industrial and economic explosion.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:25.479
Obviously, all of that went hand in hand with
an equally fierce demand for energy.

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:33.710
And being able to meet such a high demand
with so much interannual growth is quite complicated.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:37.930
Energy production plants take time to build
and go into service, however China needed

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:39.900
the energy, it was an immediate demand.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:45.830
They couldn’t wait for months, or even years
until the plants were ready.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:51.120
That’s precisely why the Chinese government
promoted laws that would allow companies and

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.300
regional governments to anticipate demand
and propose the construction of many more

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:59.190
coal plants than necessary in advance of China’s
continued explosion.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:03.680
Because, after all, the growing trend in energy
demand was clear, so they were definitely

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.020
going to need more energy plants at their
disposal, right?

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:11.920
Well, relatively recently, in 2014 the central
government went one step further and allowed

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.170
its provinces to approve and build power plants
completely independently.

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.540
That is, it decentralized decision making.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.260
And this... wasn’t a very good idea...

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:24.839
NOT if we’re talking about phasing out coal.

00:09:24.839 --> 00:09:34.130
You see, the rulers of China’s provinces
are usually people trying to step into the

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:35.430
national political sphere.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:39.350
They are career politicians with an eye on
reaching high positions in the central government.

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:42.000
That’s where the power, money and influence
are.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:48.750
But to do so they have to show certain skills,
for example a remarkable management capacity.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:54.370
In the Chinese model, it’s the final results
that count, so they have to get their province

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:57.560
to develop, if possible faster than the other
provinces.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:01.930
Therefore, provincial governments do everything
they can to support the industrialization

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.140
of their regions.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:11.260
This industrialization, naturally requires
a lot of energy… and that means coal.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:15.870
It all seems like a good recipe for success,
as long as we forget about the environment

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:20.070
and the people living there because pollution
in China’s cities is a real problem.

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:24.230
And of course, faced with such deterioration
– imagine China’s international image

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:28.850
amidst the climate panic – the central government
realized its mistake and, as we told you at

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:33.660
the beginning of the video, reversed the decision
to build a lot of new plants in 2016.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:38.550
However, this reversal has now been reversed
again – coal plants have once again been

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:42.880
greenlighted, largely to try to stimulate
an economy that has slowed down in recent

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.180
years.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.410
In China, every time growth slows down, the
government spends billions and billions on

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:51.690
all kinds of projects.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:56.750
And that explains the huge investment in large
coal plants and renewable energy sources.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:00.310
Literally mountains and mountains have been
covered with solar panels, crowned with wind

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:04.310
turbines, and its rivers have been filled
with dams for hydroelectric production.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.780
Can you imagine how much money all that cost?

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:08.020
Well, hold on to your hat.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:14.170
Out of the $ 289 billion that was invested
worldwide in renewable energy in 2018, China

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:18.480
invested more than $91 billion, that’s more
than 31%.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.830
Knowing this it shouldn’t surprise us to
know that some areas in China have been completely

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:28.670
transformed with a 100% man made landscape
that goes hand in hand with 100% sustainable

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:29.670
energy.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:34.530
And thanks to such artificial landscapes,
in 2019 China managed to get these great numbers.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:40.940
(AUDIO: China's renewable power capacity up
9.5% year-on-year in June - Reuters)

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:44.750
So, if you thought it wasn’t possible to
be dirty and clean at the same time, you were

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:45.810
clearly wrong.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:50.620
Just look at China, and its parallel boom
in coal plants and sustainable energy production.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:55.300
An energy mix that, if it were a person, would
be two-faced to say the least.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.080
But back to the big question...

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.089
Can China kick its coal habit?

00:11:59.089 --> 00:12:02.700
Well, let's see, and here’s the problem.

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:06.810
China’s demand for energy is growing so
quickly that despite the enormous investment

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:11.000
that the central government has made, renewables
can’t replace other more-polluting energy

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.000
sources, as they struggle to cover the growth
in demand.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.220
And that’s precisely where the Chinese government’s
other big bet comes in: nuclear energy.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:26.250
Check this out, China currently has 46 operational
reactors and another 11 under construction.

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:27.250
(CHART)

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:30.050
But that’s only the beginning.

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.649
Recently the State Nuclear Energy Technology
Corporation announced that the country will

00:12:33.649 --> 00:12:41.150
build at least 60 new nuclear reactors over
the next decade.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:45.339
To date, 43 have already been planned, approved
and have the necessary financing to be built.

00:12:45.339 --> 00:12:46.339
(CHART)

00:12:46.339 --> 00:12:50.350
And that's not all, an additional 136 projects
have been formally proposed to be analysed.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:54.730
This makes sense because we’re talking about
a country where nuclear energy isn’t taboo.

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:59.360
In fact, we can find initiatives as curious
as that of the China General Nuclear Power

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:04.830
Group (CGN), which even invited couples to
celebrate their weddings at the Daya Bay nuclear

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:06.280
power plant in Shenzhen.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:13.280
Of course, in exchange they had to post their
wedding photos online.

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:18.330
China is betting heavily on nuclear energy
to complement its renewable energy sources.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.671
And it’s important to keep in mind that
with its current hunger for energy, nuclear

00:13:21.671 --> 00:13:26.740
energy looks like the only possible option
for the Asian giant if it is to reduce its

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:30.029
dependence on coal.

00:13:30.029 --> 00:13:33.510
Anyway, now it’s your turn:

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:34.510
What do you think?

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:39.020
Can China effectively abandon coal, stop building
plants and start dismantling them while its

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:40.560
industry continues to grow?

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:44.930
Will China ever shake its image as an environmentally
hostile place and be seen as an example of

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:45.930
clean energy?

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:49.800
What do you think of the decision to reverse
the shutdown of the new coal plants, which

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.680
goes against China's commitments in the Paris
Agreement?

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:54.220
Leave your answer in the comments.

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:58.830
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.089
subscribe for brand new videos.

